WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:06.890
On the election campaign trail in Abuja, Nigeria's
capital, Chike Ukaegbu is visiting a private

00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:07.890
school.

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:19.480
He's the youngest-ever Nigerian presidential
candidate, and education is his central theme.

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:23.720
Ukaegbu lived in the US, where he worked as
a mathematics professor.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:31.329
He's calling for 35 per cent of the country's
budget to go towards education.

00:00:31.329 --> 00:00:37.020
The school's principal is surprised to see
a politician who understands education.

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:42.500
The school receives no state support and is
financed by fees affordable only to those

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:59.660
earning good money.

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:09.549
The 
political failures of the past few decades

00:01:09.549 --> 00:01:12.079
can be seen three hours' drive from Abuja.

00:01:12.079 --> 00:01:19.100
In Kaduna, in north Nigeria, is a state-funded
school barely worthy of the name.

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:24.869
Parts of it have collapsed; the classrooms
have neither desks nor chairs.

00:01:24.869 --> 00:01:34.549
Abdul is 14 years old - himself a student
but currently responsible for his whole class.

00:01:34.549 --> 00:01:41.020
"I take care of them because I'm older and
want to help my brothers and sisters like

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:42.020
this.

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:45.250
The teachers aren't here again, that's why
I'm doing it."

00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:50.899
Here, there are no books, jotters or chalk.

00:01:50.899 --> 00:01:58.099
Abdul's only learning aid is a whip.

00:01:58.099 --> 00:02:04.709
It's 9am - and only now has the first teacher
shown up for work.

00:02:04.709 --> 00:02:12.420
The school has only four teachers for 800
students.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:17.060
Muhammadu Hassan's sons go to school here
- a fact their father rues daily.

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:20.080
The teachers are poorly paid, he says.

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:23.820
No one would want to teach at such a school.

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:27.500
"My children should become doctors or soldiers.

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:30.860
They should have a worthwhile job and a good
future.

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:32.140
That's my dream.

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:38.060
But under these circumstances, nothing will
come of it."

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:43.050
If he had enough money, the motorbike-taxi
driver says, he would have long ago sent his

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:45.220
children to private school.

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:49.860
The ruling party of President Buhari has been
in power for five years in the federal state

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:50.860
of Kaduna.

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:55.950
The Commissioner of Education has shifted
the blame for the educational situation onto

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:57.370
previous governments.

00:02:57.370 --> 00:03:00.720
70 per cent of teachers were unqualified.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:21.350
More than 20,000 teachers have been let go
due to illiteracy.

00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:26.730
Kaduna State spends a third of its budget
on education - but, at a federal level, education

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:30.530
spending makes up only 7 per cent of the budget.

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:36.890
The issue plays no role in President Buhari's
election campaign.

00:03:36.890 --> 00:03:42.070
Chike Ukaegbu wants to change that - but he
and his small party are struggling to be taken

00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:43.600
seriously in Nigeria.

00:03:43.600 --> 00:04:05.569
In spite of a minimal campaign budget, he
wants to lay down

00:04:05.569 --> 00:04:11.990
a marker.

00:04:11.990 --> 00:04:18.310
Ukaegbu says it's a simple equation: if Nigeria,
with its booming population, no longer invests

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:22.440
in education, the next crises will not be
far away.

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:28.460
That, he says, is something every politician
should understand.

